---
import BrandArtwork from "../../components/BrandArtwork.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/ResourceLayout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How much autonomy should an agent have? — Modesto"
  description="Autonomy is not one slider. It is a set of separate permissions — read, change, run, merge — and each is worth setting deliberately."
>
  <article class="article-shell">

    <header class="article-header">
      <div class="article-eyebrow">
        <a href="/articles" class="back mono">← Blog</a>
        <div class="article-eyebrow-right">
          <button type="button" id="download-pdf-btn" onclick="window.print()" class="mono article-pdf">Download PDF ↓</button>
          <span class="mono article-date">September 5, 2026</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="article-cover article-cover-cubes"><BrandArtwork /></div>
      <div class="article-header-copy">
        <span class="mono article-cat">Research</span>
        <h1>How much autonomy<br />should an agent have?</h1>
        <p class="article-lede">
          Autonomy is usually described as a slider from "assistant" to "agent". In practice it
          is four separate decisions — what a run may read, change, execute, and merge — and
          collapsing them into one setting is where most of the trouble starts.
        </p>
        <div class="article-meta">
          <span class="mono">Modesto Team</span>
          <span class="meta-sep mono">·</span>
          <span class="mono">7 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-rule"></div>

    <div class="article-body">

      <p>
        Ask someone how autonomous their coding agent is and you will usually get a single
        answer: fairly, or not very. It is a strange way to describe the thing, because the
        question hides at least four separate permissions that have very little to do with
        each other.
      </p>

      <p>
        An agent that can read your entire repository but change nothing is enormously useful
        and close to harmless. An agent that can change three files but never run a command is
        a different tool with a different failure mode. An agent that can execute arbitrary
        shell commands in your working tree is a third thing entirely. And an agent that can
        merge its own work to a shared branch is a fourth. Lumping these into one slider means
        you end up granting the fourth in order to get the first.
      </p>

      <h2>Four permissions, not one slider</h2>

      <p>
        The useful decomposition is boring, which is probably why it gets skipped. <strong>Read</strong>
        is what the agent may see: the working tree, history, environment, secrets. <strong>Change</strong>
        is what it may write to, and where those writes land — your checkout, or an isolated
        worktree. <strong>Run</strong> is what it may execute, and whether the result of that
        execution can reach the network. <strong>Merge</strong> is whether its output can become
        the shared state everyone else builds on.
      </p>

      <p>
        These have genuinely different risk profiles. Read is mostly a confidentiality question.
        Change is recoverable as long as it is version-controlled. Run is the one that can reach
        outside the repository and do something you cannot undo. Merge is the one that makes a
        mistake everyone else's problem. Treating them as one dial means the weakest reason to
        grant permission sets the level for all four.
      </p>

      <h2>Autonomy is a property of the run, not the agent</h2>

      <p>
        The second mistake is attaching autonomy to the agent instead of the task. The same
        model that should be free to roam a scratch branch at 2am should probably not have merge
        rights on <code>main</code> during a release freeze. Nothing about the model changed
        between those two situations — the stakes did.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is why Modesto scopes permissions to a run rather than to a provider. Each turn
        records what it was allowed to do, and automations persist a permission snapshot so a
        scheduled run three weeks from now behaves the way it did when you set it up, rather
        than inheriting whatever the defaults have drifted to since.
      </p>

      <h2>The cost of getting it wrong in either direction</h2>

      <p>
        Too little autonomy has a real cost, and it is usually undercounted because it shows up
        as tedium rather than incident. If every command needs approval, you approve without
        reading by the fortieth prompt, which is worse than not asking — you have trained
        yourself to click yes and kept the paperwork that says you did not.
      </p>

      <p>
        Too much autonomy fails differently. The agent does ten things, eight are right, and the
        two that are wrong are buried in a diff large enough that nobody reads it closely. The
        failure is not that the agent acted; it is that the record of what it did was too coarse
        to audit at the speed it produced it.
      </p>

      <h2>Make the record match the autonomy</h2>

      <p>
        The way out is not to pick a level and hope. It is to make the trail proportional to the
        freedom. If an agent may run commands unattended, the run should produce something you
        can inspect afterwards without reconstructing it from memory: what changed, what was
        executed, what it declined to finish, and what it thinks should happen next.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is the reasoning behind checkpoints being real Git refs rather than chat summaries.
        A summary is a claim about what happened. A ref is the thing that happened. As you hand
        an agent more rope, the difference stops being academic.
      </p>

      <p>
        Autonomy, in other words, is not a setting you get right once. It is a pair of dials you
        move together: how much the agent may do, and how legible the result is afterwards. Move
        the first without the second and you have not built a faster team — you have built a
        faster way to produce work nobody can check.
      </p>

    </div>


  </article>
</Layout>
<style>
  .article-print-footer {
    display: none;
  }

  .article-shell {
    width: min(100% - 48px, 1440px);
    margin: 0 auto;
    border-inline: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  /* ── Header ── */
  .article-header {
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 20px clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .back {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .back:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .article-date {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-cover {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 6;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-cover img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .article-header-copy {
    padding: clamp(40px, 7vw, 80px) clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse 60% 45% at 50% -10%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--violet) 10%, transparent), transparent 68%);
  }

  .article-cat {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 7rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.07em;
    line-height: 0.88;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  .article-lede {
    max-width: 600px;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.6vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--muted);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .meta-sep {
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  /* ── Rule ── */
  .article-rule {
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
  }

  /* ── Body ── */
  .article-body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr min(680px, 100%) 1fr;
    padding: clamp(60px, 8vw, 100px) 0;
    row-gap: 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-body > * {
    grid-column: 2;
  }

  .article-body p {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-body h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-top: 64px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }

  .article-body ul {
    margin: 0 0 28px 0;
    padding-left: 24px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .article-body li {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-body blockquote {
    margin: 48px 0;
    padding: 32px 36px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper-deep) 60%, transparent);
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* ── Footer ── */
  .article-footer {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: end;
    padding: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px) clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-footer-left {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .article-footer-left > .mono {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-footer-left p {
    max-width: 480px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .article-cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: 24px;
    padding-bottom: 6px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    width: fit-content;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .article-cta:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .article-shell {
      width: min(100% - 32px, 1440px);
    }

    .article-body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      padding-inline: clamp(20px, 5vw, 40px);
    }

    .article-body > * {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .article-footer {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  /* ── Print / "Download PDF" ──
     No PDF library — reuses the browser's own print-to-PDF against the page
     content already rendered. Keeps the real theme colors/gradients instead
     of flattening to plain black-on-white, hides site chrome (nav, the ←
     Blog/Download PDF/date bar, this article's own closing footer, and the
     global site footer) so only the cover image through the end of the
     article prints, and shows a small branded footer in their place. */
  @media print {
    * {
      -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;
      print-color-adjust: exact;
      color-adjust: exact;
    }

    .site-header,
    .global-footer,
    .footer-mark,
    .capability-ticker,
    .article-eyebrow,
    .article-footer {
      display: none !important;
    }

    body {
      background: #ffffff;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-shell {
      width: 100%;
      border-inline: 0;
    }

    /* Safari's print engine is well documented to mis-render CSS Grid (and
       occasionally flex) during the print pass — tracks can collapse or
       children can lose their placement, which is what "ignores all our
       styling" looks like from the outside even though the colors/fonts
       below are applied correctly. Every layout still visible in print gets
       forced to plain block flow here so there's no grid/flex left for
       Safari to get wrong; Chrome/Firefox render identically either way. */
    body,
    .article-body,
    .article-body p,
    .article-body li,
    .article-header,
    .article-header-copy {
      font-family: "DM Sans", -apple-system, "Helvetica Neue", sans-serif;
    }

    .mono,
    .article-meta,
    .article-print-footer {
      font-family: "DM Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
    }

    .article-header {
      display: block;
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .article-header-copy {
      display: block;
    }

    .article-cover,
    .article-header-copy {
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-header h1 {
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-lede,
    .article-meta {
      color: #4a4d57;
    }

    .article-body {
      display: block;
      width: min(680px, 100%);
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding-bottom: 0;
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .article-body > * {
      grid-column: unset;
    }

    .article-body p {
      color: #2b2d33;
      orphans: 3;
      widows: 3;
    }

    .article-body h2 {
      color: #14151a;
      break-after: avoid;
      page-break-after: avoid;
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-body li {
      color: #2b2d33;
    }

    .article-body blockquote,
    .article-body ul,
    .article-body img {
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-body blockquote {
      background: #f4f4f6;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-body code {
      background: #f0f0f2;
      border-color: #ddd;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    /* Normal document flow, not position:fixed — a fixed-position footer
       gets painted at the same screen coordinate on every printed page and
       can overlap the last line or two of that page's content instead of
       repeating cleanly, which is what was making text unreadable near page
       breaks. This renders once, after the article ends. */
    .article-print-footer {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 8px;
      height: 34px;
      margin-top: 32px;
      padding: 0 4px;
      border-top: 1px solid #ddd;
      color: #6b6e78;
      font: 500 10px "DM Sans", sans-serif;
    }

    .article-print-footer img {
      width: 14px;
      height: 14px;
      object-fit: contain;
    }

    .article-print-footer span:first-of-type {
      color: #14151a;
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .article-print-footer-url {
      margin-left: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
